"Chinese medicine treats a single day as a miniature version of the full year, with the same yin-yang logic simply compressed into twenty-four hours instead of twelve months. Dawn is compared to spring: yang begins rising after its lowest point, matching the pattern of new growth. Noon is compared to summer: yang reaches its peak, the same full, outward expansion covered in an earlier unit's lesson on the season. Dusk is compared to autumn: yang begins withdrawing, gathering in the way autumn's cooler air draws things inward. Midnight is compared to winter: yin reaches its own peak, and the body is meant to be at its most still and storing.": "41a7437dfc28",
"Chinese medicine treats a single day as a miniature version of the full year, with the same yin-yang logic simply compressed into twenty-four hours instead of twelve months. Dawn is compared to spring: yang begins rising after its lowest point, matching the pattern of new growth. Noon is compared to summer: yang reaches its peak, the same full, outward expansion covered in an earlier unit's lesson on the season. Dusk is compared to autumn: yang begins withdrawing, gathering in the way autumn's cooler air draws things inward. Midnight is compared to winter: yin reaches its own peak, and the body is meant to be at its most still and storing. This isn't a poetic flourish layered on top of the science — it's the same logic this course has used throughout, applied at a smaller scale. The advice to sleep before eleven at night, covered in an earlier unit's lesson on the organ clock, lines up with this pattern precisely: eleven p.m. sits right at the entrance to the day's \"winter,\" the point where yin should be gathering strength undisturbed, exactly the way this course's seasonal unit recommended not spending yang during the year's true winter. Reading a single day this way changes what counts as \"working against the day.\" A late-morning burst of activity aligns with the day's own rising yang; the same burst of activity at midnight works against the day's yin the same way exercising heavily in the dead of winter works against the year's. The seasons, the organ clock, and this one-day yin-yang cycle are three different sizes of the exact same clock, and this course has now covered all three.": "7c2ef5bf54a1",
